I'm Prannoy. I started as a middle school math teacher in Baltimore, and every job since has been a version of that one. I founded Bansho, a K-12 learning app, grew it to a million in revenue and sold it, then spent more than four years shipping AI features at Audible. Now I shape how an AI tutor teaches. The question underneath all of it hasn't changed: did the learner actually learn? AI made that harder to answer, so I write about it.

The teaching was pre-algebra for 7th graders through Teach for America, with an education degree from Johns Hopkins on nights and weekends. Twelve-year-olds don't hand over their attention because a title says teacher. It gets earned in the first two weeks or the whole year is spent losing it, and a room of thirty only moves in one direction when the thing they're moving toward matters more than any single kid in it. I've been trying to build that same thing ever since, in products and in poems: attention earned, then pointed somewhere worth going.

The poems work the same question from the other side: what gets passed down, and what we make of it. They're published here and there and slowly collecting into a book. I can't keep the poems and the products in separate rooms, and this site is what happens when I stop pretending to.
